<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        For an RRSP, TFSA, FHSA or non-registered account, LoonieBooks keeps what the account holds on a statement date (each
        fund or stock, its market value and book value) rather than every trade. Import the latest monthly statement or the
        broker's holdings report every month or quarter; each import replaces the account's holdings and keeps its balance, and
        so your net worth, up to date. Investment accounts don't import transactions, so they aren't listed on
        <a href="import">Import</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Why holdings, not trades</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Buying and selling inside the account only swaps cash for shares. Imported as transactions, they'd show up as large
            spending and income.</li>
        <li>Dividends and interest earned inside an RRSP, TFSA or FHSA aren't your income for tax. For a non-registered account,
            your broker's T5 and T3 slips report them.</li>
        <li>Market gains and losses never appear as transactions, so trades alone can't keep the balance right.</li>
        <li>Money you put in or take out still shows up on the chequing side, as a transfer, with RRSP and FHSA contributions
            tagged for the <a href="help?a=tax-report">tax report</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Importing holdings</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Add the account on <a href="accounts">Accounts</a> with the type <span class="help-ui">Investment</span>, if you haven't.</li>
        <li>On your broker's website, download the latest monthly statement (PDF) or the holdings (or <em>positions</em>) report as
            CSV. You can also select the holdings table on the page, copy it, and paste it in.</li>
        <li>On <a href="holdings">Holdings</a>, choose the <span class="help-ui">Account</span> (<span class="help-ui">Holdings</span>
            beside the account on Accounts opens the page with it chosen). Its <span class="help-ui">Account currency</span> shows
            beside it: holdings that don't state a currency are read as that one, so set a US-dollar account to USD on
            <a href="accounts">Accounts</a> (<span class="help-ui">Edit</span>; clicking the currency takes you there) before importing. Then drag the file onto the box under it,
            or click the box to choose it, or paste the table and press
            <span class="help-ui">Read pasted text</span>. It's read on this device.</li>
        <li>From a statement, LoonieBooks reads the holdings section: the table under a header such as <em>Symbol, Quantity, Price,
            Market value</em>, including sections like <em>U.S. dollar holdings</em>, and stops at the account activity. Statements
            are laid out differently by each broker, so check what it found. If it can't find the table, use the CSV report or
            paste the table instead. Scanned statements (pictures) can't be read.</li>
        <li>Check the <span class="help-ui">Columns</span>. LoonieBooks guesses them from the header names; change any it got wrong.
            Only <span class="help-ui">Market value</span> is required (or <span class="help-ui">Quantity</span> and <span class="help-ui">Price</span>,
            which it multiplies). Total lines and section titles are left out; open the note under the count to see them.
            A row named by a currency, such as Interactive Brokers' <em>USD</em> and <em>CAD</em> cash lines, is read as cash in that
            currency: its quantity is the cash, whatever price and value the report gives it in another currency.</li>
        <li>If the report covers several accounts (Wealthsimple's does), pick which one to import. Account numbers show only their
            last four digits, and aren't stored.</li>
        <li>Check <span class="help-ui">Holdings as of</span>, the report's date (for a statement, the end of its period).</li>
        <li>Leave <span class="help-ui">Set the account balance</span> ticked to make the account's balance the holdings' total (in the
            account's currency), then
            <span class="help-ui">Save holdings</span>. A balance already set from a later date is kept.
            <span class="help-ui">Start over</span> clears what was read without saving.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>US dollars and other currencies</h3>
    <p>
        The <span class="help-ui">Exchange rate</span> beside the account is the one rate the whole ledger uses for US dollars (and for
        any other currency your holdings are in). When the page opens, LoonieBooks gets the Bank of Canada's latest daily rate,
        sending only the currency code, and uses it; <span class="help-ui">Get the latest</span> asks again. To use your own rate, for
        example your broker's, type it in. A new rate revalues every holding in that currency, and an account whose balance was its
        holdings' total gets the new total, so your net worth follows. Without an internet connection the last rate is kept. Each
        account's balance is in its own currency; net worth adds them up in Canadian dollars at these rates.
    </p>

    <h3>Today's prices</h3>
    <p>
        Between statements, LoonieBooks prices listed stocks and ETFs from <a href="https://stocktreemap.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">stocktreemap.com</a>,
        when this page or the <a href="">dashboard</a> opens (at most every 15 minutes) and when you press
        <span class="help-ui">Refresh prices</span>. It downloads the whole list of prices and looks your holdings up in it on this
        device, so nothing about your holdings is sent. Each priced holding's market value becomes its quantity times the new price, and an
        account whose balance was its holdings' total gets the new total, so your net worth follows. The time under a price is when it
        was quoted. Prices are updated every couple of hours during the trading day, so they can be up to two hours behind the market.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>A symbol without an exchange is looked up by the holding's currency: in Canadian dollars, on the TSX (then the TSX
            Venture, Cboe Canada and the CSE); in US dollars, in the US. <em>BRK.B</em>, <em>REI.UN</em> and <em>TSX:XEQT</em> are all
            understood.</li>
        <li>A price is only used in the holding's own currency, since the same symbol elsewhere is often another company.</li>
        <li>A statement without symbol or quantity columns that names the ticker in brackets at the end of the description
            (<em>REDDIT INC CDR C$HDG (RDDT)</em>) is priced by that ticker, with the quantity its value and price imply.</li>
        <li>Some holdings keep the statement's price; the note under the holdings says which and why. Mutual funds (codes such as
            <em>TDB902</em>), GICs, bonds and cash aren't in the list, and a price far from the holding's own recent one is left out
            in case it's the wrong listing, or the shares were split (import a new statement then).</li>
        <li>A price older than the statement isn't used, and the next import replaces the prices with the statement's until the
            following refresh.</li>
        <li>A price stocktreemap hasn't updated for more than five days is still used, but flagged: the listing may have stopped
            trading or left stocktreemap's list. If stocktreemap stops updating altogether, the page and the dashboard say since
            when.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Under the account's holdings, the note says when stocktreemap's prices are from and when LoonieBooks last checked, and lists
        the holdings that weren't updated and why. The <a href="">dashboard</a> shows the same for every account.
    </p>
    <p>
        A stock or ETF that isn't in stocktreemap's list (a smaller Canadian ETF, say) can be added: press
        <span class="help-ui">Ask stocktreemap.com to add</span> under the list. This one step does send something: just those
        symbols and their currency, never quantities, values, accounts or your other holdings, and only when you press it.
        stocktreemap looks each one up and adds it if it's a listing in that currency; the price is saved at once, and from then on
        it's updated with the rest. If it can't be found, the list says so, and the button isn't offered for it again for a week.
        Mutual funds can't be added this way, since they aren't listed on an exchange.
    </p>

    <h3>Options</h3>
    <p>
        A holding whose symbol names an option contract (<em>INTC 17DEC27 35 C</em> as Interactive Brokers writes it,
        <em>INTC271217C00035000</em>, or Questrade's <em>INTC17Dec27C35.00</em>) is read as contracts of 100 shares, with the price per
        share: 3 contracts at US$26.75 are worth US$8,025. A report that gave such a holding's value as quantity times price is put
        right on import, and holdings imported before are put right when prices next refresh.
    </p>
    <p>
        stocktreemap doesn't carry options, so they aren't asked about there. Instead, press
        <span class="help-ui">Get option prices from Yahoo Finance</span> under the list. From then on, each refresh sends the option
        contracts' symbols (nothing else: no quantities, values or other holdings) to this site's own server, which asks Yahoo Finance
        for their prices, since a browser can't ask Yahoo itself. This browser remembers the choice; <span class="help-ui">Stop</span>
        beside the option prices turns it off. Yahoo's option prices can be delayed, and a contract that rarely trades may show a
        price from days ago, which the list flags.
    </p>

    <h3>What the page shows</h3>
    <p>Under <span class="help-ui">Holdings in</span> the chosen account, below the import:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The account's market value, book value and gain over book value, in Canadian dollars.</li>
        <li>Its holdings with quantity, price, market value, book value, the gain over book value, and each holding's share of the
            account. Amounts in another currency show in Canadian dollars at the exchange rate, with the original amount
            underneath.</li>
        <li>How many holdings were priced from stocktreemap.com and as of when, with <span class="help-ui">Refresh prices</span>, and
            which kept the statement's price.</li>
        <li>A note when the account's balance differs from its holdings' total, for example after you edited the balance by hand.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Clear</span> removes the account's holdings (the balance stays as it is).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Choose another <span class="help-ui">Account</span> to see its holdings.</p>

    <p>
        Book value and gains are what your broker reports, for keeping track. They aren't adjusted cost base (ACB) for capital
        gains, and none of this is tax or investment advice. Holdings are included in backups; see
        <a href="help?a=your-data">Where your data is kept</a>.
    </p>
</div>
